Healthcare Digest - In the News
Executive Summary
ACA integrity policy moved from retrospective enrollment cleanup to a redesig=
ned 2028 enrollment process.
CMS estimates mandatory annual verification would reduce Exchange enrollment =
by 332,500, lower advance premium-tax-credit payments by $7.6 billion annuall=
y and modestly worsen risk pools.
Molina put numbers behind the Medicaid margin-turn thesis.
Its early 2027 view places rates near 6% against 5.5%-6% medical-cost trend=
=E2=80=94better than the recent rate shortfall, but not yet a wide margin-rec=
overy spread.
Electronic prior authorization gained a commercial-market demand signal.
Five major employers want the capabilities being built for regulated plans ma=
de available to their self-funded plans, increasing pressure on TPAs to deplo=
y common FHIR workflows across lines of business.
Two transactions advanced distinct provider-integration models.
Horizon plans to own a major New Jersey ambulatory network, while Kaiser-back=
ed Risant is adding a third health system to its reusable value-based-care pl=
atform.
DOJ created an explicit resolution path for legacy healthcare False Claims Ac=
t exposure.
The six-month pilot may accelerate internal audits, repayment decisions and t=
ransaction diligence.
Overall environment:
improved for Medicaid margin visibility and administrative-technology demand;=
 deteriorated for ACA enrollment continuity; broadly unchanged in aggregate.
1. CMS makes active verification a condition of subsidized Exchange enrollment
What happened
On October 23, CMS proposed requiring Exchanges to verify household income an=
d size, citizenship or eligible noncitizen status, other qualifying coverage =
and residency before providing advance premium-tax-credit payments or cost-sh=
aring reductions. Starting with 2028 coverage, existing members would have to=
 return annually and confirm their information to retain financial assistance=
; otherwise, they could be automatically re-enrolled only without subsidies. =
The first proposed pre-open-enrollment verification period would begin August=
 1, 2027. (
CMS,
Exchange Pre-Eligibility Verification Proposed Rule
, October 23, 2026
) (
cms.gov
)
CMS estimates that the proposal would reduce annual Exchange enrollment by ap=
proximately 332,500, decrease APTC spending by $7.6 billion beginning in 2028=
 and increase average premiums by 0.4%, or $2.89 per member per month, becaus=
e administrative barriers would disproportionately deter younger and healthie=
r consumers. (
Federal Register public-inspection document,
CMS-9873-P
, October 23, 2026
) (
public-inspection.federalregister.gov
)
Implication
This extends the September membership-removal thesis into a permanent annual =
operating model. Marketplace carriers now face more churn, delayed subsidy ac=
tivation, retroactive enrollment transactions and less predictable risk pools=
=E2=80=94not merely tighter broker controls. Premium revenue should decline w=
ith enrollment, although CMS acknowledges that claims may also fall and does =
not quantify the net margin effect.
The near-term technology demand is for eligibility orchestration, document co=
llection, identity and residency verification, broker outreach and enrollment=
 reconciliation. Providers face a smaller but meaningful 2028 increase in cov=
erage gaps, bad debt and delayed care if eligible consumers fail to complete =
verification.
Watch next
Whether CMS reduces friction for passive renewals and how Exchanges operation=
alize retroactive subsidies and pended plan selections without generating avo=
idable claims and premium-reconciliation errors.
2. Molina quantifies a narrow Medicaid rate-trend crossover
What happened
Molina reported a 93% third-quarter Medicaid medical-cost ratio and said elev=
ated medical trend appears to be plateauing as recent state rate actions begi=
n to close the funding gap. Management=E2=80=99s preliminary 2027 assumptions=
 place Medicaid rates at approximately 6% and medical-cost trend at 5.5%-6%; =
it also expects enrollment pressure from work requirements and more frequent =
eligibility reverifications. (
Molina Healthcare,
Third Quarter 2026 Financial Results
, October 21, 2026
;
earnings-call transcript, October 22, 2026
) (
molinahealthcare.gcs-web.com
)
Elevance provided corroborating, though less specific, evidence: premium incr=
eases offset membership attrition, while Medicaid enrollment fell 4.9% year o=
ver year. Elevance=E2=80=99s consolidated benefit ratio improved primarily be=
cause of Medicare Advantage and individual ACA performance, so its release do=
es not establish a fully recovered Medicaid margin. (
Elevance Health,
Third Quarter 2026 Results
, October 21, 2026
) (
sec.gov
)
Implication
The new evidence changes the Medicaid thesis from =E2=80=9Crates should event=
ually catch up=E2=80=9D to =E2=80=9Crates may approximately match trend in 20=
27.=E2=80=9D That is an inflection, not normalization: a zero-to-50-basis-poi=
nt spread leaves little protection against pharmacy, outpatient or acuity sur=
prises.
Plans should continue prioritizing acuity detection, eligibility forecasting,=
 payment integrity and high-cost case management rather than assuming broad m=
argin expansion. Providers should expect continued contracting discipline eve=
n where state rates improve, because membership attrition and mix changes can=
 offset nominal rate gains.
Watch next
Whether fourth-quarter utilization remains within Molina=E2=80=99s 5.5%-6% tr=
end assumption and whether state rate updates include retrospective relief or=
 only prospective increases.
3. Self-funded employers push prior-authorization infrastructure beyond CMS-0=
057
What happened
CMS and the Department of Labor announced on October 21 that Amazon, Axon, Go=
ogle, Meta and Oracle support making the capabilities developed under the 202=
5 Prior Authorization Pledge available to self-funded employer plans. Those c=
apabilities include FHIR-based electronic prior authorization, fewer services=
 requiring authorization, greater transparency and more real-time decisions. =
The employer statement is voluntary and does not itself impose deadlines or p=
erformance standards. (
CMS,
Leading Employers Back Expansion of Electronic Prior Authorization to Self-Fu=
nded Health Plans
, October 21, 2026
) (
cms.gov
)
Implication
The regulated-plan API build is becoming a commercial platform expectation. L=
arge employers do not want providers switching between electronic workflows f=
or Medicare Advantage or Medicaid patients and manual workflows for employees=
 administered by the same carrier or TPA.
This strengthens demand for reusable payer APIs, authorization-rule engines, =
clinical-document exchange and workflow integration. However, the immediate r=
evenue opportunity remains dependent on TPA contract amendments and employer =
purchasing decisions; a pledge without adoption metrics should not be treated=
 as equivalent to CMS-0057 compliance.
Watch next
Whether national TPAs commit to common deployment dates and disclose self-fun=
ded authorization volumes, decision times and real-time approval rates.
4. Regional integration advances through both ownership and shared platforms
What happened
Horizon Mutual Holdings confirmed an agreement to acquire Summit Health New J=
ersey, including New Jersey CityMD sites, subject to regulatory review. The t=
ransaction would place a major multispecialty and urgent-care network under t=
he state=E2=80=99s dominant Blue plan. (
BINJE,
Horizon BCBS to Purchase Summit Health in Billion-Dollar Deal
, October 21, 2026
) (
binje.com
)
Separately, Kaiser Permanente-backed Risant Health signed a definitive agreem=
ent to add INTEGRIS Health as its third member system after Geisinger and Con=
e Health. INTEGRIS operates 10 acute and 10 specialty hospitals and will reta=
in its brand and leadership while adopting Risant=E2=80=99s value-based-care =
and technology capabilities. (
INTEGRIS Health,
INTEGRIS Health to Become Part of Risant Health
, October 21, 2026
) (
integrishealth.org
)
Implication
Horizon is pursuing direct capture of referral, ambulatory and site-of-care e=
conomics; Risant is scaling a common operating platform across locally brande=
d systems. Both models increase enterprise standardization and favor vendors =
capable of systemwide deployment, but they also concentrate purchasing author=
ity and raise displacement risk for point solutions.
Watch next
Regulatory conditions on Horizon=E2=80=99s acquisition and evidence that Risa=
nt can reproduce its claimed operating improvements outside Geisinger and Con=
e=E2=80=99s markets.
5. DOJ puts a defined price range on voluntary FCA resolution
What happened
DOJ and HHS launched a six-month healthcare False Claims Act self-disclosure =
pilot on October 22. Companies must provide relevant facts and documents, des=
cribe remediation and repay the government using a 1.25-to-1.6 multiplier. In=
 exchange, participants may receive a coordinated release under the federal F=
CA and specified HHS administrative authorities. Submissions are accepted thr=
ough April 22, 2027; potential criminal conduct follows separate disclosure p=
rocedures. (
Department of Justice,
Healthcare False Claims Act Self-Disclosure Pilot Program
, October 22, 2026
) (
justice.gov
)
Implication
The program gives plans, providers and acquirers a more calculable alternativ=
e to waiting for whistleblower litigation or enforcement. It is particularly =
relevant to unsupported coding, risk-adjustment deletions, billing errors and=
 successor liabilities discovered during integration.
Expect stronger demand for retrospective claims analytics, coding validation,=
 defensible sampling and transaction diligence. The pathway reduces uncertain=
ty=E2=80=94not liability=E2=80=94and rewards rapid remediation before an issu=
e expands in duration, value or senior-management involvement.
Watch next
Whether DOJ accepts disclosures involving conduct already subject to payer au=
dits or government data analysis, and how coordinated releases address succes=
sor liability.
What I'm Watching Next
October 27, 2026:
CMS-9873-P is scheduled for publication in the
Federal Register
. (
federalregister.gov
)
April 22, 2027:
DOJ=E2=80=99s healthcare FCA self-disclosure pilot closes to submissions. (
justice.gov
)
August 1, 2027:
Proposed first pre-open-enrollment verification period begins for 2028 Exchan=
ge coverage, if finalized. (
cms.gov
)
Bottom Line
The most important weekly change is CMS=E2=80=99s proposed conversion of subs=
idized Exchange enrollment into an annual active-verification process with qu=
antified enrollment and risk-pool consequences. ACA enrollment continuity req=
uires the closest attention. The October 27 publication of CMS-9873-P begins =
the formal process most likely to determine whether the 2028 integrity regime=
 becomes a manageable verification workflow or a material new source of cover=
age churn.
Recent Earnings Highlights
Elevance (
ELV
)
+16.5% Last 3m
Elevance Health remains one of the leading health insurers in the US, providi=
ng medical benefits to 45 million medical members at the end of 2025. The com=
pany offers employer, individual, and government-sponsored coverage plans. El=
evance differs from its peers in its unique position as the largest single pr=
ovider of Blue Cross Blue Shield branded coverage, operating as the licensee =
for the Blue Cross Blue Shield Association in 14 states. Through acquisitions=
, such as the Amerigroup deal in 2012 and MMM in 2021, Elevance's reach expan=
ds beyond those states in government-sponsored programs, such as Medicaid and=
 Medicare Advantage plans, too. It is also an emerging player in pharmacy ben=
efit management and other healthcare services.
Reported:
October 21, 2026 =C2=B7
Google Finance earnings page
Earnings Call Summary
Elevance Health reported strong Q3 2026 results with adjusted EPS of $6.99, b=
eating expectations. Operating revenue rose nearly 1% to $50.4B, driven by pr=
emium yields and CarelonRx revenue. The company raised its full-year 2026 adj=
usted EPS guidance to at least $29.00 and increased its earnings baseline to =
at least $26.75.
At a Glance
Elevance Health Beats Q3 Expectations:
Elevance Health reported a Q3 2026 adjusted EPS of $6.99, significantly beati=
ng the estimated $4.695, and posted an operating revenue of $50.44 billion, w=
hich surpassed the expected $49.16 billion.
Strong Operating Performance Across Portfolios:
The company's benefit expense ratio improved 70 basis points to 90.6%, driven=
 by operating outperformance in Medicaid, Medicare Advantage, and Individual =
ACA lines.
Management Lifts Full-Year Guidance Outlook:
Elevance Health raised its full-year 2026 adjusted EPS guidance to at least $=
29.00 and increased its baseline to at least $26.75, reaffirming a target of =
12% adjusted EPS growth for 2027.
Carelon Segment Boosts Top-Line Gains:
The Carelon services segment reported a 5% increase in operating revenue to $=
19.3 billion, with operating gains rising 17% to $900 million.
Non-Operating Items Overstate Core Beat:
Analysts noted a quality caveat in the performance, as roughly $1.85 per shar=
e of the earnings beat stemmed from non-operating benefits like investment va=
luation adjustments.
Quest Diagnostics (
DGX
)
+6.2% Last 3m
Quest Diagnostics is a leading independent provider of diagnostic testing, in=
formation, and services in the US. The company generates over 97% of its reve=
nue through clinical testing, anatomic pathology, esoteric testing, and subst=
ance abuse testing with specimens collected at its national network of roughl=
y 2,400 patient service centers, as well as multiple doctors offices and hosp=
itals. The firm also runs a much smaller diagnostic solutions segment that pr=
ovides clinical trials testing, risk-assessment services, and information tec=
hnology solutions.
Reported:
October 22, 2026 =C2=B7
Google Finance earnings page
Earnings Call Summary
Quest Diagnostics reported strong Q3 2026 performance, with revenues growing =
10% year-over-year and adjusted EPS up over 15% to $3.00, beating estimates o=
f $2.875. Growth was driven by sustained demand across physician, hospital, a=
nd consumer channels, alongside productivity gains from automation and AI. Co=
nsequently, management raised full-year 2026 revenue and earnings guidance.
At a Glance
Quest Diagnostics Beats Earnings Estimates:
Quest Diagnostics reported Q3 2026 adjusted EPS of $3.00, beating the estimat=
ed $2.875, and posted total revenues of $3.097 billion, which surpassed the e=
stimated $3.052 billion.
Strong Requisition Volumes Drive Growth:
The company's organic requisition volume surged 14.4% year-over-year, which h=
elped power a 10% overall revenue increase driven by strong demand across phy=
sician, hospital, and consumer channels.
Full Year Guidance Raised Across Board:
Management raised its full-year 2026 outlook, now expecting adjusted diluted =
EPS to range between $11.25 and $11.35 and revenue to reach between $12.065 b=
illion and $12.105 billion.
Consumer Health Growth and Apple Expansion:
Quest's consumer health channel is expected to grow more than 30% for the ful=
l year, supported by an upcoming integration allowing direct lab ordering thr=
ough the Apple Health app.
AI and Automation Boost Margins:
Adjusted operating margin expanded 40 basis points to 16.7%, driven by produc=
tivity initiatives from automation and AI that helped offset wage inflation.
Molina Healthcare (
MOH
)
+5.9% Last 3m
Molina Healthcare Inc provides medical insurance plans through Medicaid, the =
individual exchanges, and Medicare. The company operates in four reportable s=
egments consisting of: 1) Medicaid; 2) Medicare; 3) Marketplace; and 4) Other=
. It manages health benefit risks for more than 5 million people, with more t=
han 85% of those members coming through contracts with state governments for =
their Medicaid programs. Medicaid contracts in four states-California, New Yo=
rk, Texas, and Washington-account for over half of its enrollees.
Reported:
October 22, 2026 =C2=B7
Google Finance earnings page
Earnings Call Summary
Molina Healthcare reported Q3 2026 adjusted EPS of $0.89 on premium revenue o=
f $10.5B, delivering a consolidated medical cost ratio (MCR) of 92.9% and an =
adjusted pre-tax margin of 0.6% amid a challenging medical cost environment. =
Performance across Medicaid (93% MCR), Medicare (93% MCR), and Marketplace (9=
2.5% MCR) segments was in line with expectations.
At a Glance
Molina Exceeds EPS and Revenue Estimates:
Molina Healthcare reported a reported revenue of $11,082,000,000, outperformi=
ng the estimated revenue of $10,859,186,010, and generated an adjusted EPS of=
 $0.89 which beat the consensus estimate of $0.795.
Medical Care Ratio Remains Elevated:
The company's consolidated Medical Care Ratio (MCR) stood at 92.9%, up slight=
ly from 92.6% in the prior year's third quarter due to elevated medical costs=
, though its Medicaid MCR of 93% met internal expectations.
Full-Year Guidance Reaffirmed Amid Transitions:
Molina reaffirmed its full-year 2026 adjusted EPS guidance of at least $5.25 =
and full-year premium revenue of approximately $42 billion, despite accountin=
g for a $1.50 per share impact from its new Florida Medicaid contract launch.
Strategic Exit from MAPD Business:
To address profitability pressures, management announced the company will ent=
irely exit its traditional Medicare Advantage Prescription Drug (MAPD) busine=
ss in 2027 to narrow focus exclusively on the higher-performing dual-eligible=
 population.
Strong Preliminary Building Blocks for 2027:
Management introduced an early 2027 framework projecting adjusted EPS buildin=
g blocks to exceed $9.50 per share, supported by an anticipated 6% average Me=
dicaid rate increase and contract maturity.
Stock Dropped on Margin Pressures:
Molina Healthcare's stock dropped roughly 3.5% following the announcement as =
investors reacted to a lower preliminary 2027 premium outlook and tight 0.6% =
adjusted pre-tax margins caused by the ongoing medical cost squeeze.
